1
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,400
Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI) is an injury that disrupts the brain and its activity

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,800
TBI can result from a blow to the head, a jolt of the brain, or an object that goes through the skull

3
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Each of these heads represents 20,000 people

4
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:41,000
Of the 1.7 million injured, 50,000 people die, and more than 250,000 are hospitalised

5
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:50,000
TBI can be mild to severe; severe TBI can lead to permanent disability and even death

6
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:57,000
75% of brain injuries are mild, meaning they are not life-threatening

7
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,000
In the US, most brain injuries in children, 55%, are caused by falls.

8
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:10,000
24% are caused by being hit in the head with an object, like a soccer ball

9
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Among children older than five, car accidents are the #1 cause of TBI-related death

10
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:21,000
Assualt is the #1 cause of TBI-related death in those younger than five

11
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:27,000
But even a mild brain injury, like a concussion, can seriously affect daily activities

12
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:37,000
Mild TBI can cause problems with the child's speaking or understanding, movement, thinking and memory, and personality or mood

13
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:45,000
We can all help to reduce the risk of TBI in children: use a safety seat and/or safety belt when a child is in a motor vehicle

14
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:53,000
Make sure the child wears a helmet when riding a bicycle, skateboarding, and playing sports like hockey and football

15
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,000
Install window guards and stair safety gates at home

16
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:01,000
Avoid shaking or jolting a baby

17
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:01,000
To learn more, visit www.nichd.nih.gov